{"major":{"id":"UCLA-LINGUISTICSANDANTHROPOLOGYBA","name":"Linguistics and Anthropology BA","kind":"major","catalog_year":"2026-27","source":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/major/2026/LINGUISTICSANDANTHROPOLOGYBA","sections":[{"name":"Preparation for the Major","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"ANTHRO 4","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 20","status":"inactive"},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the equivalent to the sixth level of one foreign language or the equivalent to the third level of two different foreign languages."}],"label":"Preparation for the Major"}},{"name":"Honors Program","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 198A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 198B","status":"inactive"}],"label":"Linguistics 198A and 198B"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 199","status":"inactive"}],"label":"Linguistics 199"}],"label":"Honors Program","notes":"Departmental honors are awarded at graduation to those students who have a grade-point average of 3.6 or better in their junior and senior years and who have received a grade of A in Linguistics 198A and 198B or in 199. Qualified students may be proposed by any member of the faculty to the faculty as a whole for the award of highest honors on the basis of a piece of research in linguistics completed at UCLA."}},{"name":"The Major","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 102","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 103","status":"inactive"}],"count":1,"label":"Linguistics 102 or 103","notes":"Select one course from:"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 110","status":"inactive"}],"label":"Linguistics 110"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 119A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 120A","status":"inactive"}],"count":1,"label":"Linguistics 119A or 120A","notes":"Select one course from:"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 120B","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 127","status":"inactive"}],"count":1,"label":"Linguistics 120B or 127","notes":"Select one course from:"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING M146","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"ANTHRO M150","status":"active"}],"count":1,"label":"Linguistics M146","notes":"Select one course from:"}],"label":"Required Linguistics Courses"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"ASL 131","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 114","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 120C","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 144","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 160","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 161","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 170","status":"inactive"}],"count":2,"label":"Linguistic Elective","notes":"Select two courses from:"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"ANTHRO 151","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"SOCIOL CM124A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"COMM M144A","status":"active"}],"count":1,"label":"Anthropology 151 or Sociology CM124A","notes":"Select one course from:"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"text","text":"Anthropology 130 through 139"}],"label":"Anthropology 130 series","notes":"Only one course may apply."},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"text","text":"Anthropology 150 through 159"}],"label":"Anthropology 150 series","notes":"Only one course may apply."},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"text","text":"Anthropology 160 through 169"}],"label":"Anthropology 160 series","notes":"Only one course may apply."},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"SOCIOL CM124A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"COMM M144A","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"SOCIOL CM125","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"COMM M125","status":"active"}],"label":"Sociology CM124A or CM125"}],"count":3,"label":"Upper-Division Electives","notes":"Select three courses from the following options:"}],"label":"The Major"}},{"name":"Computing Specialization","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10B","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10C","status":"active"}],"label":"Program in Computing"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 32","status":"active"}],"label":"Computer Science"}],"count":1,"label":"Introduction to Computing","notes":"Select one series from:"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 185A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 61","status":"inactive"}],"label":"Linguistics 185A and Mathematics 61"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING C104","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 127","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 132","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 165A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 165B","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 165C","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 180","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 185B","status":"inactive"}],"count":1,"label":"Additional Linguistics Elective","notes":"Select one course from:"}],"label":"Computing Specialization","notes":"Students may select a specialization in Computing by (1) satisfying all the requirements for a bachelor’s degree in the specified major and (2) completing Program in Computing 10A and 10B and 10C (or Computer Science 31 and 32), Linguistics 185A, Mathematics 61, and one course selected from Linguistics 104, 127, 132, 165A, 165B, 165C, 180, 185B. Students graduate with a bachelor’s degree in their major and a specialization in Computing."}}],"narrative":[],"overrides_applied":[]},"nodes":[{"id":"ANTHRO 4","title":"Culture and Communication","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"required","groups":["Preparation for the Major"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, one hour. Required as preparation for both bachelor’s degrees. Introduction to study of communication from anthropological perspective. Formal linguistic methods compared with ethnographically oriented methods focused on context-bound temporal unfolding of communicative activities. Topics include language in everyday life and ritual events, socialization, literacy, multilingualism, miscommunication, political discourse, and art-making as cultural activity. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/ANTHRO4"},{"id":"ANTHRO 151","title":"Ethnography of Everyday Speech","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"upper","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: Anthropology 151 or Sociology CM124A"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 4","requires":{"type":"course","code":"ANTHRO 4","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; fieldwork. Designed for juniors/seniors. Course has two interrelated objectives: (1) to introduce students to ethnography of communication—description and analysis of situated communicative behavior—and sociocultural knowledge that it reflects and (2) to train students to recognize, describe, and analyze relevant linguistic, proxemic, and kinesic aspects of face-to-face interaction. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/ANTHRO151"},{"id":"ANTHRO M150","title":"Language in Culture","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"upper","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: Linguistics M146"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 4 or Linguistics 20","requires":{"type":"or","items":[{"type":"course","code":"ANTHRO 4","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},{"type":"course","code":"LING 20","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null}]},"ambiguous":false,"description":"(Same as Linguistics M146.) Lecture, three hours; discussion, one hour; fieldwork, two hours. Study of language as aspect of culture; relation of habitual thought and behavior to language; and language and classification of experience. Holistic approach to study of language, with emphasis on relationship of linguistic anthropology to fields of biological, cultural, and social anthropology, as well as archaeology. (Core course for linguistics field.) P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/ANTHROM150"},{"id":"ASL 1","title":"Elementary American Sign Language","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":false,"role":"prerequisite","groups":[],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, five hours. Introduction to fundamentals of American sign language. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/ASL1"},{"id":"ASL 131","title":"Structure of American Sign Language","units":"4","offered":[],"level":"upper","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["The Major: Linguistic Elective"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 1 or Linguistics 20","requires":{"type":"or","items":[{"type":"course","code":"ASL 1","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},{"type":"course","code":"LING 20","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null}]},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, two and one half hours. Advanced overview of the linguistic analysis, research methods, and topics of American Sign Language. Major topics include phonology, morphology, syntax, language use, and linguistic applications. Examination of some comparisons with English and other spoken and signed languages. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/ASL131"},{"id":"COM SCI 31","title":"Introduction to Computer Science I","units":"4","offered":["Fall"],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Computing Specialization: Computer Science"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, four hours; discussion, two hours; outside study, six hours. Introduction to computer science via theory, applications, and programming. Basic data types, operators and control structures. Input/output. Procedural and data abstraction. Introduction to object-oriented software development. Functions, recursion. Arrays, strings, pointers. Abstract data types, object-oriented programming. Examples and exercises from computer science theory and applications. Letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMSCI31"},{"id":"COM SCI 32","title":"Introduction to Computer Science II","units":"4","offered":["Fall"],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Computing Specialization: Computer Science"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 31","requires":{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, four hours; discussion, two hours; outside study, six hours. Object-oriented software development. Abstract data type definition and use. Overloading, inheritance, polymorphism. Object-oriented view of data structures: stacks, queues, lists. Algorithm analysis. Trees, graphs, and associated algorithms. Searching and sorting. Case studies and exercises from computer science applications. Letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMSCI32"},{"id":"COMM M125","title":"Talk and Social Institutions","units":"4","offered":[],"level":"upper","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["The Major: Sociology CM124A or CM125"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"(Same as Sociology CM125.) Lecture, four hours; discussion, one hour. Designed for juniors/seniors. Practices of communication and social interaction in number of major institutional sites in contemporary society. Setting varies but may include emergency services, police and courts, medicine, news interviews, and political oratory. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMMM125"},{"id":"COMM M144A","title":"Conversational Structures I","units":"4","offered":[],"level":"upper","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: Anthropology 151 or Sociology CM124A","The Major: Sociology CM124A or CM125"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"(Same as Sociology CM124A.) Lecture, three hours; discussion, one hour. Introduction to various structures employed in organization of conversational interaction, such as turn-taking, action sequencing, and repair. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMMM144A"},{"id":"COMPTNG 10A","title":"Introduction to Programming","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, two hours; laboratory, eight hours. No prior programming experience assumed. Basic principles of programming, using C&plus;&plus;; algorithmic, procedural problem solving; program design and development; basic data types, control structures and functions; functional arrays and pointers; introduction to classes for programmer-defined data types. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG10A"},{"id":"COMPTNG 10B","title":"Intermediate Programming","units":"4","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 10A or Computer Science 31","requires":{"type":"or","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null}]},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, one hour; laboratory, eight hours. Object oriented programming in C++; operator overloading; memory management, copy and move constructors, copy and move assignment operators, destructors; iterators; data structures and their implementation, linked lists, binary search trees; inheritance and polymorphism; recursion, algorithms for sorting and searching. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG10B"},{"id":"COMPTNG 10C","title":"Advanced Programming","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing"],"column":2,"prereq_text":"course 10B","requires":{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10B","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, two hours; laboratory, eight hours. More advanced algorithms and data structuring techniques; additional emphasis on algorithmic efficiency; advanced features of C&plus;&plus;, such as inheritance and virtual functions; graph algorithms. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG10C"},{"id":"LING 20","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"required","groups":["Preparation for the Major"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"LING 102","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: Linguistics 102 or 103"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"LING 103","titl